mpc2000xl: let the desktop DATA wheel follow vertical drag and scroll

F14/F15 made the DATA wheel unreachable on an ordinary ANSI keyboard. F9
and F10 are not substitutes here: this same matrix already assigns them to
16 Levels and Next Seq. Put one-click decrement/increment on minus/equals,
which are free and adjacent on that keyboard.

The old zero key delta was not a mouse sensitivity setting. In
analog_field::frame_update(), zero makes a digital direction add one count
only on its first frame; a non-zero delta is added on every frame the key is
held. Use one so a tap is one click and a held key repeats without jumping.
Sensitivity stays at 100: for a relative dial that is one emulated count per
mouse pixel and one per digital delta.

The artwork explained the apparent one-click limit. Its raw input tag made
render_target::update_pointer_fields() call set_value(1) while the marker was
held, overriding the relative input. The layout helper then wrote user_value,
but ioport_field::set_user_settings() only applies that value to adjusters and
setting lists, not analog dials. Remove both broken paths from this marker and
use IPT_DIAL_V, whose standard assignment is the mouse Y relative axis. A
vertical drag can now contribute every pixel proportionally instead of
holding the port at one.

SDL already exposes vertical scroll as the mouse Z relative item. Bind its
positive and negative switch views to the digital directions, rather than
binding the raw Z axis: SDL expresses one notch as 120 relative units, while
the switch feeds the one-count key delta. This needs no OSD change and keeps
one notch equal to one wheel click.

A binding-table collision check also found that A drove both Step < and Undo,
and S drove both Step > and Erase. Keep A/S on the step pair and move the two
editing controls to the previously unused Backspace/Delete keys so every key
has one unambiguous panel action.
